---
/**
 * Pest library — paper wasps and yellowjackets, Polistes spp. / Vespula spp.
 *
 * THE ARGUMENT: urgency is a function of proximity and traffic, not of a nest
 * existing. AgriLife says so in its own words and the page is built on it:
 *  - "If not too close to areas of human activity, yellowjacket nests should
 *    be left alone." (AgriLife leaflet)
 *  - School IPM objective: "reduce human encounters with the wasps, but not to
 *    eliminate them from the entire area since they are beneficial predators."
 *  - Paper wasps "feed on insects, including caterpillar pests, and thus are
 *    considered to be beneficial insects by many gardeners."
 *
 * VERIFIED:
 *  - Paper wasp: 3/4 to 1 inch, slender, narrow-waisted, smoky black wings
 *    folded lengthwise at rest; "reddish-orange to dark brown", often yellow
 *    markings. Texas species include P. exclamans and P. carolina.
 *  - Yellowjacket (V. squamosa): workers ~1/2 inch, clear wings, black with
 *    yellow markings on head/thorax/abdomen; "The body is not hairy."
 *  - TAIS honey-bee separator: yellowjackets are "hairless and won't be seen
 *    carrying pollen". TAIS also: baldfaced hornet is "a type of yellowjacket
 *    and not a hornet".
 *  - NEST SHAPE is the fastest call. Paper wasp: "a single paper-like comb of
 *    hexagonal cells", "suspended by a single filament", open from below.
 *    Yellowjacket: enclosed, entrance hole only; "spherical… a number of round
 *    combs, attached one below another, and surrounded by a many-layered outer
 *    cover".
 *  - GROUND NESTING: four separate Texas A&M sources. "Yellowjackets are
 *    primarily ground nesters, but they also construct aerial nests."
 *  - QUEEN CYCLE: fertilised queens overwinter in cracks/crevices/under bark,
 *    "frequently enter homes and offices, especially during warmer periods".
 *    "In late summer, queens stop laying eggs and the colony soon begins to
 *    decline." Max colony size Aug–Sep; males and next year's queens Oct–Nov.
 *  - NEST REUSE: "New nests are constructed every year, often in the same
 *    general location…" and for yellowjackets "Abandoned nests rapidly
 *    decompose and are not reused the next year… so-called perennial colonies
 *    may be maintained" in sheltered sites or mild winters.
 *  - Paper wasp colony: "20 to 30 adults and rarely more than 200 cells".
 *  - TIMING: "Treat in the late evening when most wasps have returned."
 *  - PROHIBITION, verbatim: "Gasoline should never be poured into underground
 *    nest holes. This dangerous practice creates a fire hazard, contaminates
 *    the soil."
 *  - AAAAI 2016: systemic reactions 3% of adults, 0.4–0.8% of children; large
 *    local ~10% (range 2.4–26.4%); "at least 40 deaths each year" from insect
 *    sting anaphylaxis; ~50% (25–75%) recurrence after a prior systemic
 *    reaction; after a cutaneous-only systemic reaction ~10% chance of a
 *    future systemic reaction, <3% chance of a more severe one.
 *  - CDC/NCHS 2011–2021: 788 deaths from hornet, wasp and bee stings, average
 *    72/year, range 59 (2012) to 89 (2017), 84% male.
 *
 * ⚠️ FOUR HONESTY FLAGS THAT MUST SURVIVE ANY EDIT:
 *  1. SPECIES. Which Vespula predominates in the Llano Estacado / Permian
 *     Basin is NOT VERIFIED. AgriLife's Texas species page covers the southern
 *     yellowjacket; BugGuide's western yellowjacket range includes Texas
 *     without specifying where. State ground-nesting behaviour (well
 *     supported, species-independent). DO NOT name a regional species.
 *  2. COLONY SIZE CONFLICT. "Up to 4,000 workers" (texasinsects) vs "up to 45
 *     levels of combs and 20,000 adult workers" (AgriLife leaflet). Fivefold
 *     apart. Almost certainly typical-annual vs exceptional-perennial. Cite
 *     the RANGE with the perennial caveat. Do not blend or average them.
 *  3. TWO DIFFERENT DEATH FIGURES. CDC ~72/year counts all hornet/wasp/bee
 *     sting deaths; AAAAI "at least 40 each year" counts insect sting
 *     anaphylaxis. Different definitions and ascertainment. Never present as
 *     the same statistic and never add them.
 *  4. PESTICIDE ACTIVES. The AgriLife leaflet names carbaryl, esfenvalerate
 *     and permethrin dusts for ground nests. Registrations and labels change
 *     and that leaflet is dated. This page does NOT name actives. The label on
 *     the product in hand governs, and that is a licensed applicator's call on
 *     the day, not a website's.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AnswerBox from '../../components/AnswerBox.astro';
import Crumbs from '../../components/Crumbs.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import Cta from '../../components/Cta.astro';

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Pest library', path: '/pest-library/' },
  { name: 'Paper wasps and yellowjackets', path: '/pest-library/paper-wasp/' },
];

const quickAnswer =
  'Texas A&M’s position is that a yellowjacket nest not close to human activity should be left alone, because these are beneficial predators. Urgency comes from where the nest is, not from the nest existing. Colonies peak in August and September and die out after the first hard freeze, and next year’s nest is built new — the location repeats, the nest does not.';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Is it a paper wasp or a yellowjacket?',
