  },
  {
    q: 'Do yellowjackets really nest in the ground here?',
    a: 'Yes, and it is the norm rather than the exception. AgriLife states that yellowjackets are primarily ground nesters but also construct aerial nests, and lists underground nests in gardens, flower beds, pastures and roadside embankments. Three other Texas A&M sources say the same. What we will not do is tell you which species it is — the question of which yellowjacket predominates on the Llano Estacado or in the Permian Basin is not something we could source, and the behaviour is what matters for what you do about it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When is a nest actually urgent?',
    a: 'When it is close to where people go. AgriLife’s own words are that if not too close to areas of human activity, yellowjacket nests should be left alone. So: a nest at a doorway, walkway, play area, meter, HVAC unit or mailbox is urgent. A nest anywhere a mower or trimmer will pass is urgent, because that is how ground-nest stinging incidents usually happen. A nest inside a wall void or attic with interior access is urgent. A sting-allergic person on the property makes anything close urgent. A small paper wasp comb high under an eave at the back of the house, away from traffic, is not.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I just wait it out?',
    a: 'Often, yes, and knowing the calendar is what makes that a decision rather than a gamble. Colonies reach maximum size in August or September, then produce males and next year’s queens in October and November, and after the first hard frost most yellowjacket nests die. If it is late in the season and the nest is not in your way, waiting is a real option that AgriLife supports. If it is June and the nest is over the back door, waiting means living with a colony that has not peaked yet.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I knock down last year’s nest?',
    a: 'It will not prevent anything, but it is not pointless either. AgriLife states that abandoned yellowjacket nests rapidly decompose and are not reused the next year, and that new paper wasp nests are constructed every year — often in the same general location as last season’s. So removing an empty winter nest is cosmetic. The useful part is the location: that spot told you where next spring’s queen is likely to build, which makes it the place to watch in April and the place to seal if it is a void or a gap.',
  },
