    q: 'How bad is a sting, really?',
    a: 'Painful, usually local, occasionally serious, rarely fatal — and it is worth having the actual numbers rather than a vague warning. Allergy specialists put potentially life-threatening systemic reactions at about three percent of adults and under one percent of children, with large local reactions around ten percent. Nationally, the CDC recorded 788 deaths from hornet, wasp and bee stings across 2011 to 2021, an average of seventy-two a year, and eighty-four percent of those were men. If you have had a systemic reaction before, the risk of another on a future sting is around half, which is the number that should send someone to an allergist rather than to a website.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there anything I should definitely not do?',
    a: 'Do not pour gasoline into a ground nest. AgriLife’s school IPM guidance says it in as many words: gasoline should never be poured into underground nest holes, because the practice creates a fire hazard and contaminates the soil. It is a persistent rural remedy out here and it is a genuinely dangerous one. The other thing not to do is treat an aerial nest in the middle of the day — AgriLife’s timing guidance is late evening, when most of the wasps have returned to the nest.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Paper Wasps & Yellowjackets | West Texas Pest Library"
  description="Which nests are urgent and which can wait for the freeze, why yellowjackets nest in the ground, and the sting numbers as the allergy literature reports them."
  path="/pest-library/paper-wasp/"
  quickAnswer={quickAnswer}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <Crumbs items={crumbs} />

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Pest library · <em>Polistes</em> and <em>Vespula</em> spp.</p>
      <h1>Paper wasps and yellowjackets</h1>
      <div class="measure"><AnswerBox text={quickAnswer} /></div>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Almost every page you will read about wasps starts from the assumption that a nest is a problem
          to be removed. Texas A&amp;M AgriLife Extension does not start there, and neither will we. Its
          leaflet states that if not too close to areas of human activity, yellowjacket nests should be
          left alone. Its school pest management plan sets the objective as reducing human encounters with
          the wasps rather than eliminating them from the area, on the explicit grounds that they are
          beneficial predators. And its paper wasp material notes that wasps feed on insects including
          caterpillar pests, which is why many gardeners count them as useful.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is an extension service telling you that the default answer is not treatment. We are a pest
          control company and we are still going to publish it, because the alternative — implying that
          every nest on a property is a job — is the reason people do not trust pest control companies.
        </p>
        <p>
          What the page is actually for is the decision underneath: this nest, in this spot, at this time
          of year, with these people walking past it. That has a real answer, it changes month to month,
          and it is worth getting right.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Identify the nest, not the insect</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>Paper wasp nest: open, one comb, one stalk.</strong> A single paper-like comb of
          hexagonal cells, suspended by a single filament, with the cells visible from below. Under eaves,
          in porch corners, inside the lip of a patio umbrella, in a grill you have not opened since May,
          in a mailbox, under the rail of a deck. If you can see the cells and the developing larvae in
          them, it is a paper wasp.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Yellowjacket nest: enclosed, one hole.</strong> AgriLife describes it as spherical,
          made of a number of round combs attached one below another and surrounded by a many-layered
          outer cover, with an entrance hole. You cannot see the combs. Most of the time you cannot see the
          nest at all, because it is underground and the only visible sign is a stream of insects going in
          and out of a hole in the dirt.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The insects, if you get a look.</strong> Paper wasps run three-quarters of an inch to an
          inch, slender and distinctly narrow-waisted, with smoky black wings held folded lengthwise when
          at rest — that folded-wing posture is a good tell at a distance. Colour is reddish-orange through
          dark brown, often with yellow markings. Texas species include one that is brown with yellow
          markings and one that is reddish-brown overall.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Yellowjacket workers</strong> are about half an inch with clear wings, black with
          characteristic yellow markings on head, thorax and abdomen, and — the detail worth memorising —
          the body is not hairy.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And the honey bee question, because it comes up every spring.</strong> The Texas Apiary
          Inspection Service gives the cleanest separator: yellowjackets are similar in size to honey bees,
          about half an inch, but they are hairless and will not be seen carrying pollen. A honey bee is
          visibly fuzzy and often has pollen loads on its legs. This is not pedantry. A honey bee colony is
          a live removal for a beekeeper, not something to spray, and mistaking one for the other has a
          real cost. The same source also points out that the baldfaced hornet is a type of yellowjacket
          rather than a hornet, which explains why its nest looks like a yellowjacket nest hung in a tree.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Ground nests, which is what makes this dangerous rather than annoying</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>Ground nesting is the norm, and four Texas A&amp;M sources say so.</strong> The AgriLife
          leaflet states plainly that yellowjackets are primarily ground nesters but also construct aerial
          nests. Its species page says nests are most often underground, occasionally in wall voids and
          indoors. The apiary inspection service describes them as primarily terrestrial. The school IPM
          plan lists the ground first among nest sites. Underground nests turn up in gardens, flower beds,
          pastures and roadside embankments; aerial ones in trees, under eaves, in wall voids, in open
          garages and sheds, on porches and in abandoned furniture.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>One thing we are not going to tell you.</strong> Which yellowjacket species predominates
          out here is not something we could establish from a source we trust. AgriLife's Texas species
          page covers the southern yellowjacket; the western yellowjacket's published range includes Texas
          without saying which part of it. Since the behaviour — ground nesting, defensive when disturbed,
          annual colony cycle — is the same either way, naming a species would add confidence without
          adding information.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Why the ground nest is the one that hurts people.</strong> An aerial nest announces
          itself. A ground nest does not, and the way most people find one is with a mower, a string
          trimmer, a shovel or a boot. That is a disturbance directly on top of a colony, which is exactly
          the circumstance AgriLife describes when it notes that defending workers can inflict multiple
          stings. The severity of a yellowjacket incident is usually about the number of stings, and the
          number of stings is usually about not knowing the nest was there.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Which makes late-summer property walking genuinely worth doing.</strong> If you have
          acreage, a caliche edge, a shelterbelt, an irrigated strip or an embankment, and you are about to
          mow or clear in August or September, spend ten minutes watching the ground first. Traffic in and
          out of a hole is obvious once you are looking for it, and it is a much better way to find a
          colony than the alternative.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The calendar, which decides most of the answer</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>Spring: one queen, and a nest the size of a coin.</strong> Colonies are founded in spring
          by queens that spent the winter in sheltered hiding places. A yellowjacket queen starts a nest of
          twenty to forty-five cells on her own, and the first workers emerge in about thirty days. This is
          the cheapest, safest, least eventful point in the whole year to deal with a nest in a bad
          location, and almost nobody calls then, because there is nothing to see yet.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Summer: the colony builds.</strong> Workers take over the foraging and the queen switches
          to laying. This is when the nest becomes noticeable and when the traffic at a ground hole becomes
          obvious.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>August and September: peak.</strong> AgriLife states that maximum colony size is attained
          by August or September. This is the period when a nest near a doorway is at its most serious, and
          it is also when the wasps become most interested in human food and rubbish, which is why the
          school guidance sets an action threshold around wasps observed near food or picnic tables.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>October and November: the succession.</strong> Peak is followed by the emergence of males
          and next year's queens. In late summer the queen stops laying and the colony begins to decline.
          The new queens mate and leave to overwinter under loose bark, in cracks and crevices, and
          occasionally in attics and similar sheltered spots.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>After the first hard freeze: it is over.</strong> Most yellowjacket nests die, and
          AgriLife notes that abandoned nests can then be plugged up, with or without treatment. The
          founding queen dies and the workers die with her.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The exception that keeps this honest.</strong> AgriLife is explicit that occasionally, in
          sheltered sites or during mild winters, a nest may not be abandoned — and in those cases
          so-called perennial colonies may be maintained. Mild winters are not unusual here. A nest that
          was there last year and is active again in March is not necessarily a new nest in the same spot,
          and it is worth treating as a bigger problem than a first-year colony.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>How big does a colony get? The sources disagree, so here is the range</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>Paper wasps stay small.</strong> AgriLife gives the typical mature paper wasp nest as
          twenty to thirty adults and rarely more than two hundred cells. That is a scale you can look at
          and reason about, and it is part of why a paper wasp comb away from traffic is genuinely
          leave-able.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Yellowjackets are where the numbers get strange.</strong> AgriLife's Texas species page
          gives southern yellowjacket colonies of up to four thousand workers, with some southern colonies
          reaching six feet across and surviving several years. The AgriLife leaflet describes nests
          varying from a few inches to six feet or larger, containing up to forty-five levels of comb and
          twenty thousand adult workers.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Those two figures are five times apart and we are not going to average them.</strong> The
          most likely explanation is that they describe different things — a typical annual colony against
          an exceptional perennial one that survived a mild winter and kept building. That reading fits the
          perennial-colony note exactly. But it is our reading, and rather than pick a number and present
          it as settled, the honest version is the range with the caveat attached: a normal season's colony
          runs to the low thousands; a nest that has overwintered can be an order of magnitude worse; both
          figures come from Texas A&amp;M and they do not agree.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What that means when you are standing in front of one.</strong> Do not estimate a ground
          colony by the size of the hole. There is no reliable relationship, and the difference between the
          two published figures is the difference between a nuisance and a genuine hazard. If it is
          September, it is near a walkway, and the traffic in and out is heavy, treat it as the bad case.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Stings: the actual numbers, and what they should change</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>The normal case.</strong> AgriLife describes paper wasp stings as typically causing
          localised pain and swelling, and the leaflet describes intense pain with swelling and redness at
          the site. That is what most stings are, and most of them need ice and time.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The population figures, from the allergy literature.</strong> Potentially
          life-threatening systemic reactions affect about three percent of adults and between four and
          eight in a thousand children. Large local reactions — swelling well beyond the sting site, which
          is alarming but not the same thing as anaphylaxis — run around ten percent, with published
          estimates ranging widely, from about two percent to a quarter.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The number that should actually change behaviour.</strong> After a previous systemic
          reaction, the risk of another one on a future sting is approximately fifty percent, with a
          published range of a quarter to three-quarters. That is not a small number, and it is the reason
          somebody with a history of a systemic reaction should be talking to an allergist about evaluation
          and an emergency plan rather than reading a pest control page. There is also a more reassuring
          figure alongside it: after a systemic reaction that was skin-only, the chance of a future
          systemic reaction is about ten percent and the chance of a more severe one under three percent.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Deaths, with the two figures kept separate.</strong> The CDC recorded 788 deaths from
          hornet, wasp and bee stings in the United States across 2011 to 2021 — an average of seventy-two
          a year, ranging from fifty-nine in 2012 to eighty-nine in 2017 — and eighty-four percent of those
          deaths were among men. The allergy practice parameter separately states at least forty deaths
          each year from insect sting anaphylaxis. Those are different counts of different things: all
          stinging-insect sting deaths versus deaths attributed to sting anaphylaxis, using different
          methods. They are not contradictory and they must not be added together or used
          interchangeably.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The honest summary.</strong> Stings are common and painful. Serious systemic allergy is
          real and affects a small minority. Deaths are rare — dozens a year across a country of over three
          hundred million — and heavily skewed towards men, which probably says more about who is on the
          mower and up the ladder than about biology.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Urgent, or can it wait?</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          This is the section the page exists for. AgriLife's own line is the starting point: if not too
          close to areas of human activity, yellowjacket nests should be left alone. Everything below is
          our application of that to the situations people actually call about.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Deal with it now:</strong> a nest at or beside a doorway, on a walkway, near a play area,
          at the meter, on or in the HVAC unit, in the mailbox. A ground nest anywhere you are about to mow
          or trim. A nest in a wall void or attic where there is any interior access — those wasps can end
          up inside the living space, and a void nest is not a wait-and-see. Anything close to human
          activity where somebody on the property has a known sting allergy. A large, busy ground nest in
          late summer beside anywhere people go.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>It can probably wait:</strong> a small paper wasp comb high under an eave at the back of
          a house, away from where people pass. A ground nest in a far corner of acreage that nothing will
          disturb. Almost any nest after a hard freeze, since the colony is already finished. A nest in
          October that is out of the way, because the calendar is about to solve it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The threshold nobody expects from a pest control company.</strong> AgriLife's school plan
          gives a numeric one — a single staff complaint, or five or more wasps observed near food or
          picnic tables — as the trigger for action in a school. The reason we like it is that it is a
          threshold at all. It concedes that a wasp in the area is not automatically an event. Most
          properties would be better served by a threshold like that than by a standing instruction to
          treat everything.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Doing something about it, including the thing never to do</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>Never put gasoline in a ground nest.</strong> AgriLife says it in as many words: gasoline
          should never be poured into underground nest holes, because the practice creates a fire hazard
          and contaminates the soil. It is a persistent remedy in rural West Texas, it is passed down as
          folk knowledge, and it is dangerous in two directions at once. We are publishing the prohibition
          verbatim because that is the fastest way for it to be believed.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Timing is late evening.</strong> AgriLife's guidance is to treat when most of the wasps
          have returned to the nest. The school plan specifies evening or very early morning, when children
          are absent and temperatures are cooler. Treating an active nest in the middle of a hot afternoon
          means the foragers are out, will come back to a disturbed nest, and are defensive when they do.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Aerial paper wasp nests.</strong> AgriLife describes rapid knock-down aerosols applied so
          the nest is thoroughly soaked. It also gives a non-chemical option that people are often
          surprised by: nests can be dislodged from eaves with high-pressure water from a good distance,
          and the wasps will eventually abandon the site. That works best early in the season on a small
          comb and it is a genuinely reasonable homeowner move.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Ground nests are a dust job, not a spray job.</strong> The published method is an
          insecticidal dust delivered into the entrance with a crank or power duster or a tube — the point
          being to get material into the nest rather than around the hole. AgriLife's leaflet names
          specific actives; we are deliberately not repeating them here, because that document is dated,
          pesticide registrations and labels change, and the product label in the applicator's hand governs
          on the day. That is a licensed applicator's decision, not a website's.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Prevention that actually does something.</strong> Screens on windows and exterior doors.
          Tight-fitting lids on outdoor bins — late-season yellowjackets are scavengers and rubbish is what
          brings them into the areas people use. Sealing the voids and gaps where last year's nest was,
          since AgriLife notes new nests are built in the same general locations. And a look under eaves,
          in grills, in mailboxes and in stored furniture in April, when a nest is a coin-sized comb and
          one queen.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What we will tell you on the phone.</strong> If it is a small comb in a back corner in
          May, we will say so. If it is a ground nest in September beside the front walk, that is worth
          doing properly and it is worth doing in the evening. And if the nest is empty and it is January,
          we will tell you that knocking it down is tidying rather than pest control — the useful bit is
          remembering where it was.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Common questions</h2>
      <Faq items={faqs} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <Cta
    heading="A nest somewhere it should not be?"
    body="Tell us where it is, what the nest looks like and how close it is to where people walk. Those three things decide whether this is urgent or whether the freeze will handle it, and we will tell you which it is."
  />
</BaseLayout>
